Role of Lattice Vibration in Terahertz-region for Proton Conduction in 2D Metal-Organic Frameworks
DOI: 10.1039/x0xx00000x
We studied the relation between proton conductivity and terahertz-regime vibrations of two-dimensional MOFs. The spectroscopies clarified the collective motion in terahertz region in 2D layers plays an essential role for efficient $\Hat{\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ conduction. Abinitio calculations suggest the collective motion is dominated by the valence electronic structure depending on metal ions.
Solid state ion conductors are widely demanded for battery, fuel cell, electrochemical sensors and electrochemical catalysis. Design and elucidation of ion conductive path in solid structures have therefore been a significant challenge with related to these applications.1 Ion conduction generally proceeds by ion hopping to neighboring coordination sites, and succeeding local structural relaxation. It is known that the processes are often dominated by lattice vibration below 10 THz ( $333(m^{-1})$ ).2, 3 Indeed, ion conductivity in some representative inorganic materials correlates with the terahertz vibration. $(x-A\mathsf{g l}$ , for example, ${\mathsf{A}}{\mathsf{g}}^{+}$ behave like in liquid-state and conduct as a consequence of sub-lattice melting where the corresponding sub-lattice vibration modes are around $5{\cdot}30~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ .4 For ${\mathsf{C s H S O}}_{4}$ and ${\sf B a c e O}_{3}$ as typical proton $\left(\mathsf{H}^{+}\right)$ conductors, the collective vibration modes of protonic species (i.e. ${\mathsf{H S O}}_{4}^{-}$ and lattice $\mathsf{O H^{-}}$ , respectively) are observed in $58{-}170\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $320{-}378~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ .2 In recent, ion conductivity for molecular-based crystals are rapidly developing.5 Among these, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising class of materials for various ion conductivities.6 Researchers control the ion conductivity by use of guests in the channels, redox activity in metal centers, ligand dynamics or acidity/basicity in the frameworks. Although there are many reports on the synthesis of ion-conductive MOFs, the relationship between ion conductivity and lattice vibration is rarely observed. Several reports describe the unique lattice dynamics and functions of MOFs in terahertz region,7 and it is significant to elucidate the correlation of lattice dynamics and ion conductivity in MOFs by spectroscopy. For the purpose, we employed three isostructural MOFs having two-dimensional (2D) ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ conductive pathway, and characterized their lattice vibration in terahertz region to describe the mechanism of ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ conductivity. We also carried out ab-initio calculations to analyze vibrational mode characteristics and to study the contribution of metal ions to the lattice vibration.
We prepared three 2D MOF having different metal ions as shown in Figure 1. The formula of the compounds is $[\mathsf{M}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{4})_{2}(\mathsf{T r H})_{2}]$ where $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n}^{2+}.$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{T r H}}=1,2,4-$ triazole. These were reported and we denote them as ZnTr, CoTr, MnTr, respectively.8 Crystal structure of $Z n\mathsf{T r}$ is shown in Figure 1A and 1B, and the parameters of crystal structures of CoTr and MnTr are summarized in Table S1. All the three compounds have same crystal systems and space groups (Orthorhombic-Pbcn). ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{-}$ coordinates to metal ion axially to form hydrogen bond network in the $a b$ -plane, and the extended H-bonds with ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{-}$ motion provides the long-range $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ hopping through the layer. The differences of ${\mathsf{O}}({\mathsf{H}}){-}{\mathsf{O}}$ distances in the hydrogen bonds between ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}$ groups are less than 0.046 and $0.073\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ for along the $\pmb{a}$ - and $b$ -direction respectively. The Xray results indicate there is negligible difference for the crystal structure in three compounds.
We checked the amount ratio of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}^{-}$ and metal ions in ZnTr, CoTr, and MnTr by ICP-AES, and these were ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{\mathsf{\bar{\Psi}}}$ −/metal ion $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ 1.58, 1.63, and 1.55. The smaller amount of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{-}$ compared with the stoichiometric amount in the crystal structure is because of the site defect of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{-}$ as described previously,9 and the amounts of structural defect for the three compounds are also comparable. Therefore, the $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ conductivity for these structures are attributed from the mobility of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{-}$ groups in the layers.
Figure 1C shows the plots of anhydrous $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ conductivity under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The conductivity of ZnTr is the highest and the values at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ are $4.6{\times}10^{-4}$ (ZnTr), $1.9{\times}10^{-6}$ (CoTr) and $9.1{\times}10^{-8}\ \mathsf{S}\ \mathsf{c m}^{-1}\ (\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}$ Tr). Total activation energy of ZnTr is 0.50 eV which is a half of those of CoTr and MnTr (1.06 and $\mathsf{1.17}\mathsf{e V})$ . Although the crystal structure and composition for these MOFs are identical, the observed $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ conductivities are significantly different which implies other factors to dominate the conductivity.
Infra-red (IR) spectroscopy measurements at $27^{\circ}C$ were carried out to investigate the characteristic of the hydrogen bonds (Figure 1D). Region I and III are intramolecular modes of TrH and ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}$ tetrahedra, and region II is $0{\-}\mathsf{H}$ modes. We assigned three broad bands observed in 1,600 to $3,100{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ to the $0{\-}\mathsf{H}$ modes. These are known as ABC bands, which are interpreted as $0\mathrm{-H}$ stretching modes in Fermi resonance with combinations involving $0\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ bending mode, and are typically seen in strong hydrogen-bonding system such as metal dihydrogenphosphates.10 Although there are two kinds of hydrogen bonds in MTr from crystal structure, we could not distinguish each other due to small difference of O–O distances.
