To investigate the lattice vibration, we performed terahertz time-domain spectroscopy (THz-TDS) at $27^{\circ}C$ (Figures 2A-2C). We also computed the theoretical spectra using ab-initio quantum mechanical calculations to identify the vibration modes (dot lines and bars in Figures 2A-2C). The calculations were performed within the framework by the density functional theory (DFT) using VASP package.11 Vibrational properties were calculated using the linear response method in the density functional perturbation theory (DFPT).12 For each normal mode, displacements of each ions and frequencies were calculated at the Brillouin zone center in a harmonic approximation. Theoretical spectra match well with experimental ones, although the frequencies are ca. $10\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ higher.
Since the optimized cell constants which were used for DFPT are a few percent larger than the experiments (Table S2), and this overestimates are attributed to the anharmonicity of the lattice vibrations of the MOFs or the limits of the exchange-correlation treatment in DFT.13 The increase of baseline in the experimental spectra indicates existence of large absorption at higher frequency region. Contrast to IR, shape of spectra in THz-TDS is different among them, which suggests that lattice vibrations or intermolecular vibrations are different. We assigned the motions of each peaks by analyzing the eigenvector of each normal modes. In the light of the motion of ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}$ tetrahedron in ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}^{-}$ which relates to structural dynamics of hydrogen bond network and ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ conduction, we found that there are three types of motions. The typical modes are illustrated in Figure 2D. They are collective rotational (R; red bar), the a- or $b$ -directing translational ( $T^{\mathsf{a}}$ or $T^{\flat}$ ; blue bar) and the $c$ -directing translational modes $T^{\mathrm{c}}.$ ; green bar) in Figures 2A-2C. Rotational mode represents each ion forming a ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}$ tetrahedron moves to rotative directions, and the translational mode represents each ion moves to same directions which are parallel to hydrogenbond network for $T^{\circ,\mathrm{b}}$ , while are perpendicular for $\pi$ . In the spectra of ZnTr, a large absorption band observed in 70 to 80 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ consisting of two vibrational modes which are assigned to $T^{\mathsf{a}}$ and $T^{\flat}$ . The other bands are $\pi$ and $R$ . In the cases of CoTr and MnTr, vibrational modes of ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}$ are composed of $T^{\mathrm{c}}$ and $R_{\sun}$ , and importantly, $T^{\mathsf{a}}$ and ${\overline{{I^{\circ}}}}$ modes are not observed in this frequency region. Thus, ZnTr which exhibits the highest $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ conductivity has all ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}$ modes in terahertz region where we measured, and this increases the degree of freedom in ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4},$ resulting in formation of a frustrated hydrogen-bond network and enhancing the mobility of ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ . We also measured the temperature dependent THz-TDS spectra (27 to $107^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , Figure S2) in order to investigate the dynamic disorder of the ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{-}$ in the lattice. The difference full width at half-maximum (FWMH) with temperature is shown. The bands corresponding to ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}$ modes of ZnTr become broader and are more sensitive to temperature than those of CoTr, which indicates the dynamic disorder of ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}$ increases in ZnTr upon heating. The behavior was reported for other inorganic ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ conductors such as solid acids, $\mathsf{C s H S e O}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C s H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P O}}_{4},$ which undergo phase transition to superionic conductivity.14 The bands of collective $\mathsf{X O}_{4}$ modes $(\mathsf{X}=\mathsf{S}\mathsf{e}$ , P) including translational and rotational motion considerably broaden by the transition into superionic phase in the literatures. The lattice modes $T^{\mathsf{a}}$ and $T^{\flat}$ in ZnTr, which are not observed in CoTr and MnTr, are accompanied by the large displacement of $Z n^{2+}$ in the ab-plane, and also by the large distortion of $\scriptstyle0-2\mathsf{n}^{2+}-0$ bond angle. As a result, hydrogen-bond network layers composed of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}^{-}$ and $Z n^{2+}$ -TrH layers oscillate in opposite direction as shown in Figure 2E.
To reveal the origin of the different lattice vibration on MTr, we evaluated Metal–O bond characteristics using Crystal-Orbital Hamiltonian Population (COHP) analysis.15 The COHP is bondweighted density of states between pair of adjacent atoms, which is calculated based on the corresponding Hamiltonian elements. It shows the number of states of which electrons participate in covalent bond. Although the sign of COHP indicates bonding (negative) and anti-bonding (positive) state in its implication, and the absolute value of integrated COHP (ICOHP) shows the covalent bond strength. Here, we showed the absolute value of COHP of single Metal–O pair to evaluate the covalency (Figures 3A-3C). In $Z n^{2+}-0$ bond $\dot{\mathsf{M}}\mathsf{B}_{N}$ AZrtnicTler $_{\mathrm{~,~o~}}$ ntlihne valence state below the Fermi level is signDifOiIc:a1n0.t1l0y3l9o/Cw9.SACn05d7t5h7Ae absolute value of ICOHP is $3.19\times10^{-4}\mathrm{eV}_{\cdot}$ which is quite smaller than those of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}{\mathsf{-O}}\left(2.46\ {\mathsf{e V}}\right)$ and $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}{-\mathsf{O}}$ $(9.43{\times}10^{-1}\mathsf{e V})$ . This indicates that $Z n^{2+}-0$ interaction is highly ionic whereas $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}{-}\mathsf{O}$ and $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}{-\mathsf{O}}$ interaction are covalent. The valence states for CoTr and MnTr are originated from s, $p$ and $d.$ -orbitals of metal ions, and especially the states near Fermi level have strong $d$ - character partially occupied.
These states are attributed to molecular orbitals with $e_{\mathrm{g}}$ and $t_{2\mathbf{g}}$ symmetry which are formed by interaction between $d$ -orbitals of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}$ split in octahedral crystal field, and $\pi$ -donating ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{-}$ ligands orbitals with each the same symmetry. Thus, the $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}{-}\mathsf{O}$ and $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}{-\mathsf{O}}$ bonds have covalent nature, indicating that this suppresses random mobility of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{-}$ ligands. On the other hand, fully occupied $d$ -orbitals of $Z n^{2+}$ are localized at around $-3.5\mathrm{~eV}$ and do not interact with ligand orbitals of neighboring oxygen atom in ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{-}$ (Figure S3). Since the valence state of $Z n^{2+}$ consists mainly of $s$ -orbital with $a_{\mathrm{1g}}$ symmetry, there is little orbital interaction between $Z n^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{}^{-}$ and $z_{n-0}$ bond are ionic. Owing to isotropic nature of ionic bond, $Z n^{2+}$ easily changes the coordination geometry and it allows the large distortion in $\scriptstyle0-2n^{2+}-0$ bond angle as seen in $T^{\mathsf{a}}$ and ${\overline{{I^{\mathrm{b}}}}}$ modes. In other words, the limited lattice vibration in CoTr and MnTr in the Terahertz energy region prevents the free $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ hopping attributed from the structural relaxation. This results the high activation energies (over $1.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ ). The combination of THz-TDS and COHP analysis revealed the unique and preferential ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ conductivity for ZnTr compared with CoTr and MnTr.
## Conclusions
We applied terahertz time-domain spectroscopy (THz-TDS) to elucidate the origin of ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ conductivity in 2D MOFs with relates to their intrinsic lattice dynamics. $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ conductivity is exclusively dominated by the terahertz vibration modes in the 2D structures, and the vibration behaviors depend on the connectivity of coordination bonds of metal ions and ligands. COHP analysis suggested the ionic bonding character in the $Z n^{2+}$ -based framework is the key for the high $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ conduction, because the ionic nature allows to show more isotropic vibration modes in the framework facilitating the efficient ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ hopping. The result for the first time highlights the lattice vibration of MOFs in terahertz region to be strongly correlated with ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ conductivity. This is complementary to other characterization including AC impedance spectroscopy to account for the origin of ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ conductivity in MOFs. As per we could modulate the unique vibration modes of MOFs, the work encourages further discovery or structural optimization of ionconductive MOFs and other molecular framework materials.
